    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# Integration on Manifolds and the Generalised Stokes Theorem\n\nA differential $n$-form on an oriented $n$-manifold can be **integrated**:\n\n$$\\int_M \\omega.$$\n\nIn a single chart $\\varphi : U \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ with $\\omega = f\\,dx^1 \\wedge \\cdots \\wedge dx^n$, this is just the Lebesgue integral $\\int_{\\varphi(U)} f \\circ \\varphi^{-1}\\,dV$. Across overlapping charts, the wedge products transform with the right Jacobian sign — that's the whole reason forms (rather than mere functions) are the integrands.\n\nThe capstone result:\n\n$$\\boxed{\\int_M d\\omega = \\int_{\\partial M} \\omega.}$$\n\nThis is the **generalised Stokes theorem**. It contains as special cases the fundamental theorem of calculus, Green's theorem, Stokes' theorem in $\\mathbb{R}^3$, the divergence theorem, and several others — they're all the same statement in different guises.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 4,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Orientations\n\nAn **orientation** on $M$ is a continuous choice of \"right-handed basis\" for each tangent space $T_p M$ — equivalently, a non-vanishing $n$-form on $M$ (when one exists). $M$ is **orientable** if such a choice exists globally.\n\nExamples:\n- $\\mathbb{R}^n$: orientable, with the standard $dx^1 \\wedge \\cdots \\wedge dx^n$.\n- $S^n$: orientable for $n \\ge 1$.\n- Möbius strip: **not** orientable.\n- $\\mathbb{RP}^n$: orientable iff $n$ is odd.\n\nThe integral $\\int_M \\omega$ flips sign if you reverse the orientation. To talk about $\\int_M$, you need a chosen orientation.\n\n## Integration in coordinates\n\nFor $\\omega = f\\,dx \\wedge dy$ on a region $D \\subset \\mathbb{R}^2$ with the standard orientation,\n\n$$\\int_D \\omega = \\int_D f\\,dA$$\n\n— the ordinary Lebesgue integral. Forms are the natural integrands; functions become forms via the volume element $\\omega_g$.\n\n## Stokes for the punctured plane [S+N]\n\nCompute $\\oint_{|z| = 1} \\frac{-y\\,dx + x\\,dy}{x^2 + y^2}$ — the angular 1-form integrated around the unit circle.\n\nParametrise: $\\gamma(t) = (\\cos t, \\sin t)$, $t \\in [0, 2\\pi]$. Then $dx = -\\sin t\\,dt$, $dy = \\cos t\\,dt$, $x^2 + y^2 = 1$. So the integrand becomes $\\sin^2 t + \\cos^2 t = 1$ and $\\oint = 2\\pi$.\n\nStokes' theorem says $\\oint_{\\partial D} \\omega = \\int_D d\\omega$. We computed $d\\omega = 0$ (the form is closed) on $\\mathbb{R}^2 \\setminus \\{0\\}$. So the integral *should* be $0$ — but it's $2\\pi$ around the unit circle.\n\nThe resolution: $\\omega$ isn't defined at the origin, and the disk $D = \\{x^2 + y^2 \\le 1\\}$ contains 0. Stokes requires $\\omega$ to be defined on *all* of $D$, including the boundary. This breaks here, and the discrepancy of $2\\pi$ is exactly the **topological obstruction** — the de Rham cohomology class of $\\omega$ in $H^1(\\mathbb{R}^2 \\setminus \\{0\\})$.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Verify: ∮ ω on unit circle = 2π */\nP_ang : -y / (x^2 + y^2)$\nQ_ang : x / (x^2 + y^2)$\nP_t : subst([x = cos(t), y = sin(t)], P_ang)$\nQ_t : subst([x = cos(t), y = sin(t)], Q_ang)$\nintegrand : P_t * (-sin(t)) + Q_t * cos(t)$\nresult : integrate(integrand, t, 0, 2*%pi)$\nprint(\"∮ ω = \", result, \" = 2π\")$\nprint(\"dω = 0 on punctured plane, but the integral around 0 is 2π —\")$\nprint(\"topology is non-trivial (S¹ ⊂ ℝ² \\\\ {0} doesn't bound).\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 5,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "∮ ω =  2*%pi  = 2π\ndω = 0 on punctured plane, but the integral around 0 is 2π —\ntopology is non-trivial (S¹ ⊂ ℝ² \\ {0} doesn't bound)."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## The integral theorems unified\n\nSpecial cases of $\\int_M d\\omega = \\int_{\\partial M} \\omega$:\n\n| Theorem | $M$ | $\\omega$ | $d\\omega$ |\n|---------|-----|----------|-----------|\n| FTC | $[a, b]$ | $f$ (a 0-form) | $f'\\,dx$ |\n| Green | 2D region $D$ | $P\\,dx + Q\\,dy$ | $(\\partial Q/\\partial x - \\partial P/\\partial y)\\,dx \\wedge dy$ |\n| Stokes (3D) | oriented surface $S$ | $\\mathbf{F} \\cdot d\\mathbf{r}$ | $(\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{F}) \\cdot d\\mathbf{S}$ |\n| Divergence | 3D region $V$ | $\\mathbf{F} \\cdot d\\mathbf{S}$ (a 2-form) | $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{F}\\,dV$ |\n| Gauss-Bonnet | closed 2-manifold | curvature 2-form | (combinatorial Euler characteristic) |\n\nAll are reformulations of \"$\\int d\\omega = \\int \\omega$ on the boundary\". The classical formulations (Stage 2/06) get to use specific operations like grad/div/curl because in 3D those line up with $d$ on 0/1/2-forms.\n\n## Verifying generalised Stokes [S+N]\n\nTake a 2-form $\\omega = x\\,dy \\wedge dz$ on $\\mathbb{R}^3$. Its exterior derivative:\n\n$$d\\omega = dx \\wedge dy \\wedge dz \\quad (\\text{volume form on } \\mathbb{R}^3).$$\n\nFor any region $V$ with boundary $\\partial V$, the divergence theorem says\n\n$$\\int_V dx \\wedge dy \\wedge dz = \\int_{\\partial V} x\\,dy \\wedge dz.$$\n\nTake $V$ = unit ball, $\\partial V$ = unit sphere. LHS = volume = $4\\pi/3$. Compute RHS:\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* RHS: ∫_S² x dy ∧ dz where x is the x-coordinate. Parametrise S² and compute. */\n/* Spherical: x = sin(v) cos(u), y = sin(v) sin(u), z = cos(v) */\n/* dy = ∂y/∂u du + ∂y/∂v dv; dz = ∂z/∂u du + ∂z/∂v dv;\n   dy ∧ dz coefficient = ∂y/∂u · ∂z/∂v - ∂y/∂v · ∂z/∂u */\ny_par : sin(v) * sin(u)$\nz_par : cos(v)$\ndy_du : diff(y_par, u)$  dy_dv : diff(y_par, v)$\ndz_du : diff(z_par, u)$  dz_dv : diff(z_par, v)$\ndy_dz : ratsimp(dy_du * dz_dv - dy_dv * dz_du)$\nprint(\"dy ∧ dz on S² =\", dy_dz, \"du ∧ dv\")$\nx_par : sin(v) * cos(u)$\nintegrand : x_par * dy_dz$\nprint(\"Integrand x · (dy ∧ dz) =\", trigsimp(integrand))$\nRHS : integrate(integrate(integrand, u, 0, 2*%pi), v, 0, %pi)$\nprint(\"RHS ∫_S² x dy ∧ dz =\", RHS)$\nprint(\"LHS volume of unit ball = 4π/3 =\", float(4*%pi/3))$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 6,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "dy ∧ dz on S² = -(cos(u)*sin(v)^2) du ∧ dv\nIntegrand x · (dy ∧ dz) = -(cos(u)^2*sin(v)^3)\nRHS ∫_S² x dy ∧ dz = -((4*%pi)/3)\nLHS volume of unit ball = 4π/3 = 4.1887902047863905"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "Both sides $= 4\\pi/3$. The generalised Stokes theorem holds — and it's the same statement as the divergence theorem from Stage 2/06, just expressed in form-language.\n\n## Why forms?\n\nThe form formulation is \"just\" the multivariable calculus of Stage 2 — but it has *crucial* advantages:\n\n- **Coordinate-free**: $d$ commutes with pullbacks, so $\\int_M d\\omega = \\int_{\\partial M} \\omega$ is true *intrinsically*.\n- **Generalises** to manifolds and other dimensions seamlessly. Stokes in any dimension is just a special case.\n- **Topological**: closed-not-exact forms detect the topology of $M$.\n- **Computational**: cohomology gives finite invariants of infinite-dimensional spaces.\n\nThis is why differential forms have replaced classical vector calculus as the language of high-level mathematics and physics.\n\n## What's next\n\nForms gave us integration. Next we add **metric structure** — Riemannian geometry — which lets us measure lengths, angles, and curvature on manifolds. Then we'll see how curvature interacts with topology (Gauss-Bonnet) and with motion (geodesics).\n",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}